    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p10-q15a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "A right-angled triangle has integer side lengths. If its perimeter equals $30$, then find the triangle's side lengths.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$5,12,13$.",
          "worked": "Let $a,b$ be the shorter sides and $c$ the hypotenuse. Then $a+b+c=30$ and $a^2+b^2=c^2$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(30-a-b)^2&=a^2+b^2\\\\ab-30a-30b+450&=0\\\\(30-a)(30-b)&=450.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $0<a,b<15$, the two integer factors lie strictly between $15$ and $30$. The only such factor pair of $450$ is $18,25$, giving shorter sides $12,5$ and hypotenuse $13$.\nCheck: $5^2+12^2=169=13^2$ and $5+12+13=30$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      },
      {
        "id": "p10-q15b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "A right-angled triangle has integer side lengths. If its perimeter equals $40$, then find the triangle's side lengths.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$8,15,17$.",
          "worked": "Let $a,b$ be the shorter sides. Substitute $c=40-a-b$ into Pythagoras.\n$$\\begin{align*}(40-a-b)^2&=a^2+b^2\\\\ab-40a-40b+800&=0\\\\(40-a)(40-b)&=800.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $0<a,b<20$, the factors lie strictly between $20$ and $40$. The only such factor pair of $800$ is $25,32$. Thus the sides are $15,8,17$.\nCheck: $8^2+15^2=289=17^2$ and $8+15+17=40$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      },
      {
        "id": "p10-q15c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Show that there are two different right-angled triangles with integer side lengths whose perimeter equals $60$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$15,20,25$ and $10,24,26$.\n\nBoth triples satisfy Pythagoras and have perimeter $60$.",
          "worked": "$15,20,25$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}15^2+20^2&=225+400\\\\15^2+20^2&=625=25^2\\\\15+20+25&=60.\\end{align*}$$\n$10,24,26$ gives\n$$\\begin{align*}10^2+24^2&=100+576\\\\10^2+24^2&=676=26^2\\\\10+24+26&=60.\\end{align*}$$\nThe triples are different, so they give two different right-angled triangles.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 76
      },
      {
        "id": "p10-q15d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Show that there is no right-angled triangle with integer side lengths whose perimeter equals $20$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "No such triangle exists.\n\nPythagoras and the perimeter would require $(20-a)(20-b)=200$, with both factors integers strictly between $10$ and $20$; no factor pair satisfies this.",
          "worked": "Suppose the shorter sides are positive integers $a,b$ and the hypotenuse is $c$. Since $a+b>c$, the perimeter gives $c<10$, so $0<a,b<10$.\n$$\\begin{align*}(20-a-b)^2&=a^2+b^2\\\\ab-20a-20b+200&=0\\\\(20-a)(20-b)&=200.\\end{align*}$$\nBoth factors must be integers strictly between $10$ and $20$. The positive factor pairs of $200$ are $(1,200)$, $(2,100)$, $(4,50)$, $(5,40)$, $(8,25)$ and $(10,20)$; none has both entries strictly between $10$ and $20$. Therefore no such triangle exists.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 68
      }
    ]
  },
